Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'The Secret of Willowmere village', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Garden Mysteries', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Tea and Tidings', 'text': "Before bed she wrote in her journal: Day one. The game is on. Tomorrow I walk the hedgerows and ask the questions no one else will ask. the kindness of neighbors — that is what this is about, though no one says so yet.\n\nBy noon the whole of Willowmere village was whispering. Beatrice Nettle listened at the bakery, at the post office, at the garden gate, collecting every whisper like crumbs. Each crumb led back to a missing will, and a missing will led somewhere darker.\n\nThat evening Beatrice Nettle spread her notes on the parlor table: a map of Willowmere village, a list of names, and a missing will in the center like a spider. 'Someone,' she said to the cat, 'is lying. And I mean to find out who.'"}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Missing Piece', 'text': "the vicar Mr. Aldous brought the parish records, and together they found the gap: a name missing where a name should be, a date that did not fit. a missing will was the key, Beatrice Nettle was certain; it unlocked a story someone had paid to keep shut.\n\nThat night she dreamed of Willowmere village with all its windows lit, and every window hiding something. She woke knowing the next step: confront the one person who had not been questioned — the silent partner himself.\n\nAt the riverside inn, the landlord remembered the silent partner drinking alone, turning a missing will over and over. 'Nervous hands,' he said. 'Guilty hands, if you ask me.' Beatrice Nettle asked him, and wrote it down."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'Questions at Dusk', 'text': 'the vicar Mr. Aldous kept watch from the garden gate while Beatrice Nettle asked about a missing will. the silent partner claimed never to have seen it — but described it perfectly, down to the scratch on its side. Beatrice Nettle noted that, too.\n\nAt dusk she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a missing will in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.\n\nBut Beatrice Nettle shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a missing will was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone.'}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "They walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. the vicar Mr. Aldous said nothing, but their silence was agreement. the kindness of neighbors — even on the worst night, Beatrice Nettle thought, especially then.\n\nthe silent partner appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Beatrice Nettle could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Willowmere village. 'I never meant —' began the silent partner, and could not finish.\n\nThe storm that had threatened Willowmere village all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Willowmere village. In the midst of it came a hammering at Beatrice Nettle's door: the vicar Mr. Aldous, soaked, with news — the silent partner had been seen digging near the old vicarage."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the silent partner's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Willowmere village was stern, but Willowmere village was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish.\n\nOn market day Beatrice Nettle laid it all before Willowmere village: a missing will, the letters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the silent partner stood with head bowed and did not deny a word.\n\na missing will was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Beatrice Nettle declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "the silent partner, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Beatrice Nettle read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nAnd if another mystery comes to Willowmere village — as mysteries do, like weather — Beatrice Nettle will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For the kindness of neighbors, always, in Willowmere village and everywhere.\n\nthe vicar Mr. Aldous brought flowers — 'for the best detective in the county,' they said, gruff as ever. Beatrice Nettle put them in water and told them not to be silly, but she kept the card."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

