k she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a pearl button in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.\n\nBut Mabel Fern shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a pearl button was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone.'}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "The storm that had threatened Willowmere village all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Willowmere village. In the midst of it came a hammering at Mabel Fern's door: postman Mr. Dobbs, soaked, with news — the rival shopkeeper had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nThey walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. postman Mr. Dobbs said nothing, but their silence was agreement. truth will out — even on the worst night, Mabel Fern thought, especially then.\n\nthe rival shopkeeper appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Mabel Fern could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Willowmere village. 'I never meant —' began the rival shopkeeper, and could not finish."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "That evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Mabel Fern wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. truth will out — I shall drink tea to that.\n\na pearl button was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Mabel Fern declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nThe constable did his duty, but Mabel Fern had already done hers: she had made the truth plain, so that no whisper could twist it. postman Mr. Dobbs squeezed her hand. 'Neatest case I ever saw,' they whispered."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'And if another mystery comes to Willowmere village — as mysteries do, like weather — Mabel Fern will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For truth will out, always, in Willowmere village and everywhere.\n\nOn Sunday Mabel Fern walked the hedgerows with no case at all, just the sun and the birds and the good green smell of Willowmere village. A detective, she reflected, is only a neighbor who pays attention.\n\nthe rival shopkeeper, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Mabel Fern read it twice and filed it with the case notes.'}]}

